1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,001
Welcome to the comprehensive masterclass tutorial for SnakeUSBIP version 3.0. Unlike commercial solutions like VirtualHere, which charge forty-nine dollars per server and limit the free tier to a single device, SnakeUSBIP is one hundred percent free, open source, and allows you to connect unlimited remote USB devices over LAN, Wi-Fi, or internet with certified drivers.

2
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:40,706
Turning your Raspberry Pi or Linux machine into a USB server takes just a single command. Our official one-click installer downloads kernel packages, configures the systemd daemon, and sets up an intelligent udev rule. Any USB device you plug in is automatically shared over your network with zero manual bind commands required.

3
00:00:40,706 --> 00:00:59,271
If you prefer sharing devices from a Windows PC, SnakeUSBIP Server has you covered. Its intuitive interface dynamically detects all connected local USB ports and peripherals. With a single click, you can share or unshare ports, inspect real-time logs, and keep it running quietly in the background within your system tray.

4
00:00:59,271 --> 00:01:18,486
On your client PC receiving remote devices, the official installer configures Microsoft WHQL-certified virtual drivers. It is fully compatible with Core Isolation and Memory Integrity on Windows 10 and 11. The installer automatically closes running tray instances and guarantees a clean installation without conflicting host nodes.

5
00:01:18,486 --> 00:01:37,005
When you launch SnakeUSBIP, it automatically scans your local subnet via UDP and discovers all active servers in seconds. Next to each server, you will see a live latency meter measuring ping in milliseconds. You can also add remote servers by manual IP or connect seamlessly across VPN networks like Tailscale and ZeroTier.

6
00:01:37,005 --> 00:01:56,545
In the device list, you will see the full peripheral tree with commercial names from the official vendor ID database. Simply click Connect, and Windows will recognize the peripheral as if it were plugged directly into your motherboard. You can also mark favorites to automatically reconnect on Windows startup or after power cycles.

7
00:01:56,545 --> 00:02:16,085
From the bottom status bar, you can switch performance modes on the fly. Low-Latency WSK mode uses direct kernel callbacks to eliminate input lag on gamepads and sim-racing wheels with full rumble, making it ideal for Moonlight and Sunshine. For webcams, laser cutters, or storage drives, Zero-Copy mode provides maximum throughput.

8
00:02:16,085 --> 00:02:32,561
SnakeUSBIP integrates natively with Windows 10 and 11 Toast notifications in the Action Center. You receive clear visual alerts when devices connect, disconnect, or when network links are restored. When closed, the application minimizes quietly to the system tray with negligible background memory usage.

9
00:02:32,561 --> 00:02:47,550
For power users, SnakeUSBIP offers comprehensive command-line automation. You can launch the executable minimized and pass the server IP and bus ID to attach controllers automatically before starting a game via Sunshine or Steam Deck, without ever touching the graphical user interface.

10
00:02:47,550 --> 00:03:04,443
The interface and structured logs support instant dynamic language switching between English and Spanish. SnakeUSBIP is a community-driven open-source project. Download the latest installer from our GitHub repository, support the project with a star, and enjoy unlimited USB peripherals across your network.

